50
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:57,824
Scott: For example, if you're in a bar, and it's on, everyone in the bar has phones, some number of those phones have microphones on, and will not have all the correct, you know, permission set, and could potentially send data to them, and let them know that they're, you know, they can collect data on that.

51
00:04:57,844 --> 00:05:48,969
Scott: The other thing that's likely, it could also be that and/or an anti-piracy measure, if they're sending a different signal, a slightly different signal, to different, you know, streams, it's like, you know, one per, I guess if someone has cable, right, they, it would have to be like at the cable operator, who would send a different signal to like each viewer, but if you're watching online, on like the web streaming, they could send a different one to each viewer, I guess the CDN is kind of there, but they could still, you know, do some amount of divisions, and then they could detect, aha, you know, this particular one, if, again, you would still need microphones to pick it up, but like, the new TV we have has a microphone in it, and could theoretically listen and send data back to Samsung, or whoever has it, right?

52
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:59,990
Rym: And before any of you say that sounds paranoid and crazy, that's real technology, companies use it, you can read all about it all over the internet, audio watermarking is a thing, it's a second screen advertising bullshit thing that they're doing.

53
00:06:00,790 --> 00:06:03,130
Rym: Yep, so, you know.

54
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:04,711
Rym: So yeah, my guess is either- But

55
00:06:04,731 --> 00:06:12,970
Scott: it's rare that, you know, that things like that are actually audible, they're usually using sounds that microphones can't hear,

56
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:13,670
Rym: that

57
00:06:14,131 --> 00:06:18,901
Scott: people can't hear that microphones can, so they're just doing a shitty job, the fact that you can

58
00:06:18,941 --> 00:06:41,270
Rym: hear it- it becomes more and more likely it will be destroyed by compression, because the thing that all compression does, for all audio in the world, that humans will listen to, is they prioritize frequencies that humans will perceive, and they just obliterate things that humans can't hear.

59
00:06:41,710 --> 00:07:05,970
Rym: So it is actually very hard to put an audio watermark into something that must survive multiple re-compressions, unless you put it in the range that human ears are literally the most primed to hear, and in this case, it is actually directly in the 1 to 2 kHz range, which is like the sweet spot of spoken word and human brains paying attention to sounds.

60
00:07:06,270 --> 00:07:10,110
Rym: Like, if they wanted to hide it, it could not be in a worse place.

61
00:07:10,591 --> 00:07:14,590
