273
00:22:58,580 --> 00:22:58,781
Rym: Yep.

274
00:22:59,121 --> 00:23:09,320
Rym: So this video, so I'm working on a project and I've been doing a lot of research into stage and cinema and television blocking and, like, acting prompts.

275
00:23:09,640 --> 00:23:14,700
Rym: Because I've had a few revelations about role-playing games during this research that I think I'll save for another time.

276
00:23:15,140 --> 00:23:29,940
Rym: But there are very few role-playing games, tabletop style or LARP style, that give players the kinds of prompts that directors and scripts give players that facilitate good role-playing.

277
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:33,125
Rym: Like, that's an area of research I'm digging into pretty deeply right now.

278
00:23:33,866 --> 00:23:51,880
Rym: And this video is one that I watched around blocking scenes, because you want to block a scene in a LARP or a role-playing game Very similarly to how you might block something for cinema or stage, the nuances being, is the audience primary versus is one of the players primary, that kind of thing.

279
00:23:53,241 --> 00:24:03,160
Rym: Much like how the best anime were the ones that drew not just from a history of anime and animation, but from a history of cinema and industrial design and all these other things.

280
00:24:03,461 --> 00:24:13,140
Rym: The best works in any space tend to derive from a lot of other spaces, and the works that tend to only derive from the space they're in tend to miss the mark.

281
00:24:14,300 --> 00:24:22,600
Rym: I think role-playing games can draw a lot from the way stage and cinema operate, and I haven't seen that vocabulary used, except in very, very few places.

282
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:24,983
Rym: Ghost Court is actually one of the rare exceptions.

283
00:24:25,424 --> 00:24:36,360
Rym: So this video is three examples of very precise blocking of shots in this movie from one of the early scenes in the movie.

284
00:24:36,961 --> 00:24:41,792
Rym: And it goes into, like, extreme depth of, like, let's take the silhouettes of the characters.

285
00:24:42,193 --> 00:24:43,215
Rym: What's going on here?

286
00:24:43,275 --> 00:24:44,217
Rym: What is this telling you?

287
00:24:44,257 --> 00:24:45,360
Rym: What is this telling the audience?

288
00:24:45,780 --> 00:24:50,326
Rym: And it's a really good 13-minute introduction into how powerful blocking is.

289
00:24:50,406 --> 00:25:02,080
Rym: And if you learn even a little bit about this, as Scott and I can both attest to, movies that do it poorly will be infuriating to you, and movies that do it well will be sublime to you.

290
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:03,101
Rym: Well, the thing

291
00:25:03,121 --> 00:25:21,240
Scott: about Kurosawa is that you watch any Kurosawa film, even ones that are not so good, and basically every single shot, he's either moving the camera in an interesting way, or he's got a perfect composition where you have foreground interest, midground interest, background interest, and things are moving.

292
00:25:21,340 --> 00:25:22,701
Scott: It's never, like, things sitting still.

293
00:25:22,761 --> 00:25:24,062
Scott: Something's moving, right?

294
00:25:24,483 --> 00:25:36,472
Scott: Even if it's, like, you know, just, like, some reeds, you know, blowing in the wind in the front of the scene while the rest, you know, while you're waiting for someone to walk into the shot in the background or something, right?

295
00:25:47,140 --> 00:25:53,347
Scott: So, Kurosawa and Kurosawa have really good shots, and then just sort of, like, you know, pedestrian shots in between, right?

296
00:25:53,367 --> 00:25:56,151
Scott: Like, as they get to their big scene, it's like, "Alright, here's the big scene.

297
00:25:56,171 --> 00:26:03,960
Scott: We're gonna shoot it amazingly, and then we'll just do normal movie biz, and then get to the next big scene where you should, you know, have everything composed

298
00:26:03,980 --> 00:26:04,160
Scott: amazingly.".

299
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:09,260
Scott: Kurosawa, no, every single frame is, might as well be the big scene.

300
00:26:09,340 --> 00:26:19,960
Rym: And part of what Kurosawa is doing is the movements of characters are blocked like crosses in a stage play, and you'll see I just used some words because I'm learning a lot about this for reasons.

301
00:26:21,820 --> 00:26:24,928
Scott: Well, the opening scene of that movie is basically a stage play.

302
00:26:24,948 --> 00:26:29,420
Scott: It all takes place entirely in one room until it moves to the second part of the movie.

303
00:26:29,761 --> 00:26:38,220
Scott: So, it's a lot like, you know, one of those one-room plays like "Death of the Salesman" or "The Glass Menagerie" where it's all in one spot until it's not.

304
00:26:38,220 --> 00:26:42,047
Rym: Speaking of which, as an aside there, I realize not everyone's familiar with this concept.

305
00:26:42,749 --> 00:26:48,360
Rym: Are you familiar with the concept of a drawing room play, which is where one-room plays kind of came from?

306
00:26:50,521 --> 00:26:52,566
Scott: How is it different from a drawing room play?

307
00:26:59,240 --> 00:27:14,420
Rym: And there were basically plays or scripts that were written kind of like music with the idea that you would like get the script, invite people over and act it out together, or like get some friends to act it out while other friends watch it happen.

308
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:26,120
Rym: So they were written with the assumption of you would like be able to stage this in your drawing room because everyone of means has a drawing room.

309
00:27:28,341 --> 00:27:32,435
Rym: I think there's also some inspiration to be taken for tabletop RPGs from that concept.

310
00:27:32,475 --> 00:27:33,318
Rym: But again, I digress.

311
00:27:33,338 --> 00:27:33,980
Rym: What's your thing of the day?

312
00:27:34,920 --> 00:27:35,808
Scott: Oh, my thing of the day?

313
00:27:35,889 --> 00:27:36,980
Scott: What was my thing of the day?

314
00:27:37,362 --> 00:27:39,140
Rym: It's a very thing of the day.

315
00:27:40,921 --> 00:27:41,361
Scott: Oh, yeah.

316
00:27:41,422 --> 00:27:52,520
Scott: So usually, you know, when you're trying to write words, right, and you want a different, you got a word, you have the right meaning, but you want a different word, you use a thesaurus, right?

317
00:27:52,820 --> 00:27:55,814
Scott: You call up your buddy Rogé, you're like, "Hey, what's a different word for

318
00:27:55,834 --> 00:27:56,035
Scott: this?".

319
00:27:56,055 --> 00:27:56,919
Scott: Right, Rogé?

320
00:27:58,040 --> 00:28:02,208
Scott: And, you know, thesauruses are often not that helpful, I've found.

321
00:28:03,290 --> 00:28:08,580
Scott: Sometimes, sometimes they are, but sometimes not that great, you know, because especially when words have multiple meanings.

322
00:28:08,981 --> 00:28:16,940
Scott: Thesaurus doesn't have a lot of options for the meaning you're looking for, and another meaning might be more common, and it's like you're not finding what you want.

323
00:28:18,580 --> 00:28:32,360
Scott: Another thing is that, you know, when you're doing, when you're writing, you know, any kind of written work, essay, article, book, right, you know, you fall into like this, you know, this trap of using the word "very" a lot, right?

324
00:28:32,440 --> 00:29:05,620
Scott: Very something, right, as your adjective, it's always very something, and when you say very something, that usually means that you're, the something that comes after the very, very good, you know, very cold, is, the second word is usually a very common, boring, unexciting, uninspiring kind of word, everyday kind of word, and that's fine for everyday kind of talking, but if you're trying to write something, and to sound smarter, fancier, you know, more eloquent, you don't want to say very something.

325
00:29:05,780 --> 00:29:09,103
Scott: You pretty much almost never want to say very anything, right?

326
00:29:09,423 --> 00:29:11,665
Scott: You always want to replace that with a fancy word.

327
00:29:12,066 --> 00:29:27,860
Scott: For example, instead of saying very cold, you could just say frigid, and that's just better, right, in some sense of the word better, and so this website is just a thesaurus, but it's a very good thesaurus, it's a better thesaurus, right?

328
00:29:28,280 --> 00:29:36,448
Scott: Better is a better way of saying very, I guess, anyway, what you do is it frames everything in terms of very.

329
00:29:36,668 --> 00:29:48,940
Scott: You, so you could go through what you've written, and look for every instance of the word very, and you say, alright, I need another word for very stupid, and it will say inane, or, right?

330
00:29:49,760 --> 00:29:56,680
Rym: It's like very cold, it says freezing, so now I just typed very, very cold, and it says bone chilling.

331
00:29:57,481 --> 00:29:58,905
Scott: There you go, bone chilling.

332
00:29:59,005 --> 00:30:03,920
Scott: So you could just take anything you've written, you don't need any AI, right, and just.

333
00:30:04,060 --> 00:30:05,659
Rym: Well you do, because this is an AI.

334
00:30:06,400 --> 00:30:06,542
Scott: Is it?

335
00:30:06,563 --> 00:30:07,680
Scott: I thought it was just a look up database.

336
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:08,487
Rym: You didn't read the about?

337
00:30:08,548 --> 00:30:09,860
Rym: Okay, so two points.

338
00:30:10,261 --> 00:30:11,600
Scott: I thought it was a look up database.

339
00:30:11,841 --> 00:30:15,638
Rym: It was obviously an LLM, and if you go to the about, it says this is an LLM.

340
00:30:16,701 --> 00:30:17,914
Scott: Oh, well, I didn't read that part.

341
00:30:18,520 --> 00:30:26,580
Rym: But two, you know, who made it, the guy who made it, Amir Karimi, or Karimi, I don't know the nationality, so there's multiple ways to pronounce that, sorry buddy.

342
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:33,480
Rym: Quote, as non-native English speakers, we're often advised to avoid overusing intensifiers like very.

343
00:30:33,880 --> 00:30:38,820
Rym: For example, instead of relying on generic terms, which is very big, for more descriptive alternatives like enormous.

344
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:42,028
Rym: This was aimed at English as a second language.

345
00:30:42,068 --> 00:30:49,620
Rym: people who were specifically trying to avoid saying very all the time, so they don't sound like they learn English from Duolingo like I sound if I try to speak French.

346
00:30:51,043 --> 00:30:52,212
Scott: Huh, I didn't read any of that part.

347
00:30:52,253 --> 00:30:53,180
Rym: I just used the tool.

348
00:30:53,740 --> 00:30:55,638
Scott: And it didn't look like an LLM, it looked like a.

349
00:30:56,281 --> 00:31:07,180
Rym: It was obviously an LLM because if you type anything pretty obscure, if you type something very obscure into it, it would say, wait a minute, and like take a while before it brought the results back.

