415
00:37:35,364 --> 00:37:39,860
Rym: this is actually a fundamental problem in the games industry right now.

416
00:37:41,261 --> 00:37:51,477
Scott: And what it comes down to is that, you know, it used to be that you could make a game and sell the game, and you'd make a bunch of money, and then you'd go and make another game.

417
00:37:51,657 --> 00:37:53,520
Scott: Just like people make movies, they make a movie.

418
00:37:54,060 --> 00:37:58,980
Scott: And then they sell it, and then it sort of enters its long tail era.

419
00:37:59,260 --> 00:38:00,963
Scott: They released Phantasmagoria.

420
00:38:01,043 --> 00:38:03,486
Rym: I paid like $60 to buy it out of Babbage's.

421
00:38:03,586 --> 00:38:04,087
Rym: I played it.

422
00:38:04,527 --> 00:38:05,088
Rym: It was great.

423
00:38:05,468 --> 00:38:12,758
Rym: Then they launched Phantasmagoria 2, and I paid like $60 at Media Play to buy it, and I played it.

424
00:38:13,018 --> 00:38:14,120
Rym: That's how games used to be sold.

425
00:38:15,641 --> 00:38:19,931
Scott: But at least for big, big games, the budgets have gone way up.

426
00:38:21,314 --> 00:38:23,680
Scott: And to justify the big budget, you need to make more money.

427
00:38:24,460 --> 00:38:29,191
Scott: And there's this guy, you may have seen him on YouTube Shorts and/or Twitch.

428
00:38:29,291 --> 00:38:32,940
Scott: I think it's his own company named Pirate Software, I think.

429
00:38:34,381 --> 00:38:38,005
Scott: And he just makes a cute little video game called Heart Somethin', I forget.

430
00:38:38,365 --> 00:38:46,295
Scott: And we actually have a friend who worked with him because he was at Blizzard for a long- this guy, who I'm talking about, was at Blizzard for a very long time.

431
00:38:46,315 --> 00:38:50,520
Scott: His dad was like the cinematics guy at Blizzard for a very long time.

432
00:38:51,240 --> 00:38:56,810
Scott: And then he left Blizzard and went to Amazon, which is where our friend worked with him for briefly.

433
00:38:56,830 --> 00:39:02,060
Scott: And now he's doing his own thing, and he's doing YouTube and Twitch stuff also, right?

434
00:39:03,020 --> 00:39:06,744
Scott: And this guy has one of his YouTube Shorts, he's like, "Let me tell you something.

435
00:39:07,245 --> 00:39:13,612
Scott: I worked on StarCraft II, Wings of Liberty, for this much time and for this much budget.

436
00:39:14,834 --> 00:39:18,278
Scott: And then we made money on that game.

437
00:39:19,019 --> 00:39:20,160
Scott: It was a big, big endeavor.

438
00:39:21,021 --> 00:39:27,507
Scott: And then World of Warcraft, right, started selling mounts, you know, to ride horses, right?

439
00:39:28,028 --> 00:39:28,708
Scott: Digital horses.

440
00:39:29,529 --> 00:39:40,680
Scott: And so, the first horse that they put out in World of Warcraft, people could just pay 10, 15 bucks, I don't know how much money it was, to buy this horse and ride it around in World of Warcraft.

441
00:39:41,642 --> 00:39:50,980
Scott: Blizzard made more money from the horse, just selling the horse, than they made from all of selling StarCraft II.

442
00:39:51,520 --> 00:40:01,430
Rym: Now, you have to understand, like, this moment, this guy talked about a lot of PAX Dev, like this exact thing in a bunch of different panels.

443
00:40:02,911 --> 00:40:12,460
Rym: Nobody outside of the games industry realized it yet, but the people who saw this happen, it was like a nuclear bomb was dropped right on top of games as an industry.

444
00:40:13,560 --> 00:40:14,622
Scott: Yeah.

445
00:40:14,822 --> 00:40:22,751
Scott: I mean, really, the first time this happened was when Valve mostly stopped making games and just made Steam instead.

446
00:40:23,231 --> 00:40:30,880
Scott: Most of their work is on Steam, and the games that they do still make, Counter-Strike, Dota, TF2, I guess, still exists?

447
00:40:30,880 --> 00:40:36,080
Rym: I guess, yeah, TF2 still makes plenty of money from selling hats, which are basically just mounts.

448
00:40:36,781 --> 00:40:36,981
Scott: Right.

449
00:40:37,382 --> 00:40:43,411
Scott: All of those games, they are games, are also stores, right?

450
00:40:43,551 --> 00:40:47,817
Scott: There's a Counter-Strike store, where you get Counter-Strike stuff, right?

451
00:40:47,917 --> 00:40:49,620
Scott: It's a TF2 store with hats.

452
00:40:49,680 --> 00:41:03,940
Rym: Yeah, we're not even talking about pay to win versus free to play, doesn't fucking matter, all that matters is that these games are stores, and people pay money into the game after they bought the game, irrespective of what they get out of it.

453
00:41:04,200 --> 00:41:04,401
Scott: Right.

454
00:41:04,501 --> 00:41:08,752
Scott: Steam is a store, Roblox is a store, Fortnite is a store, right?

455
00:41:08,812 --> 00:41:09,895
Scott: Marvel Snap is a store.

456
00:41:12,400 --> 00:41:16,785
Scott: Marvel Snap is absolutely a store, Magic Arena is a store, right?

457
00:41:17,385 --> 00:41:30,639
Scott: All the biggest money-making games that will carry on, continue, Minecraft is kind of a store, you know, even if things in the store are free, you know, they're just a bunch of mods made by fans or something, right?

458
00:41:30,659 --> 00:41:31,300
Scott: It's still a store.

459
00:41:31,841 --> 00:41:40,149
Scott: The big games that are gonna last and dominate are themselves stores with other games inside, right?

460
00:41:40,170 --> 00:41:49,900
Scott: It's like, Fortnite started out as just a game, but it's like, now you got like, Lego, frickin' Fortnite survival game in there, it's like there's all these other games in there, right?

461
00:41:50,120 --> 00:42:01,240
Scott: So it's like you install the Epic Store to install Fortnite, which is a store, and then in that store, you can buy the game for free, or, right, you can play the game Fortnite inside of two stores.

462
00:42:03,321 --> 00:42:16,620
Scott: And what it comes down to is that games don't make money, or at least they don't make enough money to justify any big kind of budget expenditure, right, that any big company is gonna bother with their time and resources on, right?

463
00:42:17,061 --> 00:42:31,400
Scott: Games these days, they might make enough money for someone who's making Animal Well, right, or a little indie game, or a Bellatro, or a Vampire Survivors, where you have a very small budget, a very small team, you worked hard and made this thing, a Dwarf Fortress.

464
00:42:31,860 --> 00:42:34,220
Rym: Undertale, Deltarune, stuff like that.

465
00:42:34,220 --> 00:42:34,460
Rym: Right,

466
00:42:35,021 --> 00:42:53,580
Scott: and you put it out, and you made something that was actually great, and you put it in someone else's store, right, Apple Store, Steam Store, Epic Store, and tons of people buy it, and so you individually make a lot of money, at least as far as you're concerned, right?

467
00:42:54,060 --> 00:43:11,920
Scott: But you're not making, you know, mega-millions, you're just making maybe regular millions, if you're really successful at it, you did something amazing, but that's a bad, and to a big company, single-digit millions, even double-digit millions, if it's low double digits, is not anything.

468
00:43:12,080 --> 00:43:24,020
Rym: Yep, and there's just an article I was reading this morning that I didn't want to, I didn't finish, I didn't want to bring it up in the episode deeply, but it was about how success does not save a game studio.

469
00:43:25,200 --> 00:43:25,247
Rym: No.

470
00:43:25,460 --> 00:43:30,200
Rym: Like, being successful does not in any way tie to continue to exist, or even making money.

471
00:43:32,900 --> 00:43:49,172
Scott: And so, the only thing, now, if you're a big company, and you want, you're in this industry of making games, or entertainment software, the only way that you can make money to satisfy your bigness is to make stores, you cannot make games, right?

472
00:43:59,680 --> 00:44:07,052
Rym: Think way back, my foray into being an executive producer with Date Night O and Pillow Fight, and like, that whole visual novel era, what were you trying to do?

473
00:44:07,433 --> 00:44:11,460
Rym: Make a marketplace for visual novels, that was the goal, making a game was just a path to that.

474
00:44:12,421 --> 00:44:14,540
Scott: That was smart, that was the right strategy.

475
00:44:14,680 --> 00:44:21,480
Rym: It's always the only strategy, it's a degenerate strategy in this era of late capitalism in the video game industry.

476
00:44:22,581 --> 00:44:32,080
Scott: And so the reason this is happening, right, is because, not just the bigness and the need for always to grow, and not being satisfied with a small profit.

477
00:44:32,280 --> 00:44:34,820
Rym: Because those pressures have been around since the dawn of capitalism.

478
00:44:35,660 --> 00:44:49,560
Scott: What's happening is, it used to be, and it still is in some areas, that, you know, these sort of middlemen, right, who do provide value, right, they provide logistics, visibility, distribution, right?

479
00:44:50,802 --> 00:45:03,220
Scott: You know, like a grocery store, it still today provides value in that, yeah, the company that makes food, right, they got a factory that makes cereal, it's like, they need grocery store, they can't go without it.

480
00:45:03,600 --> 00:45:14,580
Rym: There's a reason why wholesale and retail are very well-defined, or were very well-defined categories that existed in US law, like that is, that concept makes sense on some level.

481
00:45:14,620 --> 00:45:20,760
Scott: And, but grocery store also needs cereal manufacturer, otherwise they got empty shelf, right?

482
00:45:21,081 --> 00:45:24,720
Scott: And so they have it, everything is done, at least in a correct-ish way.

483
00:45:25,160 --> 00:45:35,480
Scott: We make the goods, we sell it to you, you sell it to the customer for more than that, and everyone is making money in between, right, in the correct-ish amount.

484
00:45:36,840 --> 00:45:55,400
Scott: What's happening nowadays is on the internet with digital goods, the digital goods are, of course, infinite in supply, so once you've chosen to go to a store, they don't, it's like, they don't continue to need you, to like, keep sending them more cereal box, right?

485
00:45:55,640 --> 00:45:59,340
Scott: It's like, your cereal's already in their store, you're good to go, right?

486
00:45:59,922 --> 00:46:01,647
Scott: That's the end of you, right?

487
00:46:01,707 --> 00:46:06,360
Scott: It's just, some legal agreement is all that they have to maintain, and they take a cut.

488
00:46:07,280 --> 00:46:16,600
Scott: So, and you get a cut also, so it benefits you somewhat, but it's like, whoever owns the store is controlling the eyeballs, right?

489
00:46:16,660 --> 00:46:19,700
Scott: You can't get someone to go directly to your website to buy the game.

490
00:46:20,662 --> 00:46:22,720
Rym: Unless I'm accepted in rare circumstances.

491
00:46:23,520 --> 00:46:25,664
Scott: Yeah, it's like, good luck with that, right?

492
00:46:25,704 --> 00:46:31,252
Scott: It's like, if you want to sell, if you want to make money by selling a video game, there's only a few stores, it's not like.

493
00:46:31,272 --> 00:46:36,440
Scott: there's a nationwide thousands of grocery stores frickin' everywhere, and you could be in some and not others.

494
00:46:36,500 --> 00:46:37,180
Rym: But also, there's a sea.

495
00:46:37,280 --> 00:46:39,117
Scott: Only sell to a certain region, right?

496
00:46:39,763 --> 00:46:40,540
Scott: There's a sea of games.

497
00:46:40,580 --> 00:46:48,200
Scott: It's like, there's Steam, Epic Store, and like, that's, and Nintendo Store, Sony Store, Xbox Store, and it's like, you've only got a handful, less than ten.

498
00:46:48,440 --> 00:46:49,081
Scott: But also, there's

499
00:46:49,141 --> 00:47:08,580
Rym: so many games out there, even if you go to, no matter where you go, there are so many games you could play today on a computer that cost under a dollar, and you might enjoy them, that discoverability and like, shining that light on something and telling other eyeballs to buy it, is the only thing that matters in terms of making money.

500
00:47:09,317 --> 00:47:09,460
Scott: Yeah.

501
00:47:09,640 --> 00:47:13,580
Scott: And it's, and it's not a store where it's like, okay, they have to keep restocking the shelves.

502
00:47:14,001 --> 00:47:20,320
Scott: It's like, everything on the shelves is just like infinite supply, and they can keep adding, the store only gets bigger.

503
00:47:21,404 --> 00:47:22,860
Scott: It's only more items every day.

504
00:47:22,960 --> 00:47:31,240
Scott: It's like, and everything else gets pushed to the back, and they're constantly adding new shelves to the store in this infinite space, and it's just, they keep getting full of new things.

505
00:47:31,620 --> 00:47:38,020
Scott: So, you are, who makes money in this situation, is not the people putting things on the shelves, right?

506
00:47:38,884 --> 00:47:41,740
Scott: The people who make money is only the people who own the store.

507
00:47:41,900 --> 00:47:43,679
Scott: They can now squeeze at both ends.

508
00:47:43,960 --> 00:47:47,119
Scott: It used to be, you'd only be able to squeeze to one side, right?

509
00:47:47,960 --> 00:47:52,620
Scott: The wholesaler could try to squeeze the store, and the store could try to squeeze the customer.

510
00:47:53,240 --> 00:48:00,840
Scott: Now, whoever, the person in the middle who owns the store can squeeze the customer, and they can squeeze the person making the games.

511
00:48:01,060 --> 00:48:07,040
Scott: They're squeezing both juices as the middle person, because they're in control, they're in control of everything.

512
00:48:07,160 --> 00:48:15,500
Rym: And independent of that, because they have such power, this is like straight-up Monopoly territory, in that they pick winners now.

513
00:48:15,620 --> 00:48:31,020
Rym: The store's algorithm, or what the store decides to show a random person who comes in from this infinite sea of possible products, they decide what's popular and what's not, effectively, with rare exceptions, where something breaks out into the general conversation.

514
00:48:32,220 --> 00:48:40,317
Scott: Yeah, it's like they choose, they have complete control over what is at the front of the store, and the store is so big, you can never make it to the back of the store.

515
00:48:40,357 --> 00:48:41,880
Scott: You'll die before you get there.

516
00:48:41,920 --> 00:48:45,199
Scott: So you know, a long time ago- If it's not in the first two aisles, you're not gonna see it.

517
00:48:45,380 --> 00:48:50,620
Rym: So a long time ago, there was one company that controlled all the oil, and something happened to that company.

518
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:55,139
Scott: There was a company that controlled all the telephone lines, something happened to them too.

519
00:48:55,600 --> 00:48:57,328
Rym: Used to be, that actually might have been a mistake.

520
00:48:57,368 --> 00:49:00,260
Rym: to break that up in retrospect, but that's a different kind of conversation.

521
00:49:00,260 --> 00:49:00,927
Rym: It happened.

522
00:49:02,380 --> 00:49:03,600
Rym: Or think about how movies used to be.

523
00:49:03,661 --> 00:49:04,470
Scott: I didn't say anything.

524
00:49:04,531 --> 00:49:05,199
Scott: that was not true.

525
00:49:05,400 --> 00:49:16,500
Rym: The store is the theater, in the old times, for movies, and it used to be that the people that made the movies also owned the store, and you had a different kind of monopoly that was equally toxic to society.

526
00:49:17,101 --> 00:49:18,805
Scott: Well, we do have some of those cases, right?

527
00:49:18,966 --> 00:49:24,600
Scott: At the Steam store, it's actually a little bit like that, because they do have Valve games in there, but not so much.

528
00:49:24,680 --> 00:49:26,363
Rym: Steam is like the fairest one.

529
00:49:26,383 --> 00:49:34,660
Rym: They take a big cut, and we could debate that, but look at like, the Epic store is very much the studio system store.

530
00:49:35,141 --> 00:49:38,620
Scott: Right, the Blizzard store, the Sony store, those are studio systems, right?

531
00:49:39,421 --> 00:49:45,238
Scott: It's like they're not pushing Valve games very much on Steam, because they're just as big as they are.

532
00:49:45,760 --> 00:49:50,980
Rym: Well, because the Valve games are themselves stores, and also, they print monies, they kind of don't give a shit.

533
00:49:51,782 --> 00:49:53,720
Scott: Yeah, they're always promoting some other game in there.

534
00:49:54,742 --> 00:50:02,140
Scott: You don't see Counter-Strike at the top of the Steam store unless it's got a super new update or something, like when Counter-Strike 2 came out.

535
00:50:02,320 --> 00:50:04,290
Scott: I don't think I've seen Dota in the Steam store.

536
00:50:05,717 --> 00:50:06,140
Scott: It's in there.

537
00:50:06,840 --> 00:50:12,539
Rym: Yeah, and thus, you might think this is where Ruben Scott pivoted to like, "Here's what we do about

538
00:50:12,579 --> 00:50:12,740
Rym: this.".

539
00:50:12,800 --> 00:50:14,668
Rym: They're like, "I don't fucking know anymore.

540
00:50:14,688 --> 00:50:17,117
Rym: I don't know how to deal with this, other than

541
00:50:17,137 --> 00:50:17,439
Rym: communism.".

542
00:50:18,841 --> 00:50:28,079
Scott: Yeah, the point is that as a consumer, you have to recognize what is a store, and be like, "That's a game, that's a

543
00:50:28,119 --> 00:50:28,400
Scott: store.".

544
00:50:28,901 --> 00:50:34,940
Scott: And I would suggest, if you want to keep your money safe and have more money for yourself, avoid stores.

545
00:50:35,720 --> 00:50:40,540
Scott: If you go into it, just going into a store is putting yourself at risk of spending money.

546
00:50:40,640 --> 00:50:41,263
Rym: I'd go further.

547
00:50:41,765 --> 00:50:44,920
Rym: I've applied this to all of my life right now, including video games.

548
00:50:46,422 --> 00:50:50,192
Rym: Steam is one exception, and that Steam is a store I will go to and use.

549
00:50:51,255 --> 00:50:53,000
Rym: They're less bad than the others in a lot of ways.

550
00:50:54,001 --> 00:51:00,940
Rym: I try to buy almost everything I buy that isn't a commodity direct from the person who made it, if they do D2C, direct to consumer.

551
00:51:01,340 --> 00:51:07,940
Rym: And if they don't, I try to buy those things from specialty stores that specialize in that kind of thing.

552
00:51:08,100 --> 00:51:15,700
Rym: Like, if I need audio equipment, I go to an audio specialist store to buy the thing, if I can't buy it directly from the manufacturer.

553
00:51:16,480 --> 00:51:23,540
Scott: Yeah, it's like, you know, that way... You know, every time, it's sort of like the same thing with dieting, right?

554
00:51:23,921 --> 00:51:27,760
Scott: I'm not a diet expert, and that's a huge topic for not-a-GeekNights out.

555
00:51:28,863 --> 00:51:30,277
Rym: That's not a topic for us to talk about.

556
00:51:30,887 --> 00:51:31,059
Scott: Ever.

557
00:51:31,561 --> 00:51:41,660
Scott: But one technique that is very effective is, you know, if you buy, say, candy and bring it home with you, you're going to eat the candy, right?

558
00:51:42,201 --> 00:51:45,235
Scott: So if you have, you know, if I got candy in the house, guess what?

559
00:51:45,316 --> 00:51:46,180
Scott: I'm going to eat the candy.

560
00:51:46,320 --> 00:51:48,499
Scott: We buy a bag of candy corn on Halloween, it gets eaten.

561
00:51:49,360 --> 00:51:51,477
Scott: Rym won't eat it, but I'll eat the candy corn because I like it.

562
00:51:52,661 --> 00:51:55,029
Scott: You know, if someone gives me a bag of chocolate, guess what?

563
00:51:55,069 --> 00:51:58,400
Scott: That means I am now going to eat thousands of calories of chocolate.

564
00:51:58,501 --> 00:52:01,040
Scott: I wouldn't have eaten had they not given me that chocolate.

565
00:52:01,581 --> 00:52:04,251
Scott: So the best thing you can do is don't buy it.

566
00:52:04,552 --> 00:52:06,760
Scott: If you just don't buy it, you ain't going to eat it.

567
00:52:07,362 --> 00:52:12,560
Scott: And that's already saved you, right, taking some calories out of your diet there, right?

568
00:52:12,860 --> 00:52:19,799
Scott: And it's the same thing with stores, is that if you don't go to stores and look at stuff, you're not going to buy stuff.

569
00:52:21,064 --> 00:52:22,700
Rym: So just don't go to stores.

570
00:52:23,400 --> 00:52:28,700
Rym: I need to use Battle.net to launch my stupid, god, Overwatch, even though I literally don't play any other Blizzard games.

571
00:52:29,420 --> 00:52:35,518
Rym: I have literally never clicked on anything in that interface except the play button under Overwatch.

572
00:52:36,200 --> 00:52:36,340
Scott: Ever.

573
00:52:37,861 --> 00:52:41,054
Scott: So, and if you are trying to make money, make a store.

574
00:52:41,636 --> 00:52:42,680
Scott: The opposite, right?

575
00:52:43,922 --> 00:52:48,620
Scott: You know, if you're making a game and you're just focusing on the game part, it's like, we love you.

576
00:52:48,761 --> 00:52:49,767
Scott: That's what we want.

577
00:52:49,787 --> 00:52:51,639
Scott: We want people making art and great games.

578
00:52:51,940 --> 00:52:58,480
Rym: When we talked about MagFest and MIVS, those ultra-independent games as insulate, like, that is culture.

579
00:52:58,740 --> 00:53:00,079
Rym: That is what we want to support.

580
00:53:00,861 --> 00:53:02,306
Scott: You're the hero, right?

581
00:53:02,446 --> 00:53:06,620
Scott: But you ain't going to make any money, even if your game is fucking incredible, right?

582
00:53:07,020 --> 00:53:10,320
Scott: If you put it in a store, yeah, you might make the money if you're the big hit.

583
00:53:11,181 --> 00:53:17,160
Scott: But you're going to, people are just going to be taking advantage of you because you're in their store, it's not your store.

584
00:53:17,781 --> 00:53:25,200
Scott: If you want to make the money, you have to make a store, which is going to make your game, not a game really anymore.

585
00:53:26,600 --> 00:53:35,280
Rym: Or you've got to be willing to accept that you won't make money and maybe you've got other goals or other aims in what you're creating?

586
00:53:36,680 --> 00:53:38,064
Scott: Yeah, I'm just saying.

587
00:53:38,124 --> 00:53:44,160
Scott: And if you're a big company, it's like, there's only money in the digital world in being a store, right?

588
00:53:44,660 --> 00:53:48,031
Scott: Amazon makes all the money, not the people selling the stuff on Amazon.

589
00:53:48,272 --> 00:53:50,840
Scott: eBay makes the money, not the people selling stuff on the eBay.

590
00:53:51,080 --> 00:53:54,067
Scott: Etsy makes money, not the crafty people at home.

591
00:53:54,308 --> 00:53:59,220
Scott: It's like, I'm not saying they make no money, they might be profiting, but it's like, they're not making the real money.

592
00:53:59,400 --> 00:54:01,042
Rym: But it's also not a sustainable ecosystem.

593
00:54:01,062 --> 00:54:14,220
Rym: because we're at a point to where, like, that article I referenced in the early part, it talked about how a game that had three million active players in its first month was deemed unsuccessful and, like, the studio was all dropped.

594
00:54:14,901 --> 00:54:26,460
Rym: And the argument is, if three million active players of a game can be considered by anyone to be not successful by some measure, then how the fuck is five million players going to make the difference compared to three?

595
00:54:27,080 --> 00:54:43,400
Rym: No consumer has any agency over how this goes down, and the ultimate argument of the article is that gamers who love games have no way to materially support the people who make games because there's literally no way to get that money to them short of writing them direct checks.

596
00:54:44,680 --> 00:54:44,861
Scott: Yep.

597
00:54:45,761 --> 00:55:02,980
Scott: You know, it's like, they could, like, make a website themselves with some sort of e-commerce on it, but then, even then, they gotta pay for, right, there's so much stuff that the store does for them, which is the argument for the, the legitimate argument for the existence of the store, right?

598
00:55:03,821 --> 00:55:12,080
Scott: You know, handling payments, refunds, just so many things that they're doing for you, which does justify some amount of fee for their services.

599
00:55:15,281 --> 00:55:17,843
Rym: But the stores also don't compete with each other fairly.

600
00:55:17,923 --> 00:55:22,348
Rym: It's not like the game will pick up from Valve and go to Epic.

601
00:55:22,749 --> 00:55:33,420
Rym: Those transfers between stores are difficult and uncommon, and a lot of the stores are company stores, so going over there is how you get fucked over by Sony if you're just someone trying to make games.

602
00:55:35,640 --> 00:55:38,473
Rym: The moral of this is capitalism is a cancer, I guess.

603
00:55:38,855 --> 00:55:39,879
Rym: Like, I don't know what else to say.

604
00:55:41,080 --> 00:55:49,911
Scott: But it's just, I think, I think the moral is, you know, people are still thinking about a lot of games as games, right?

605
00:55:49,971 --> 00:55:52,274
Scott: It's like, I just want people to recognize.

606
00:55:52,835 --> 00:55:54,737
Scott: Fortnite is a store, right?

607
00:55:55,298 --> 00:55:56,339
Scott: Roblox is a store.

608
00:55:56,559 --> 00:55:57,140
Scott: It's not a game.

609
00:55:58,101 --> 00:55:58,281
Scott: Right?

610
00:55:58,301 --> 00:56:02,728
Scott: There might be games in that store, but it is itself a store, right?

611
00:56:03,749 --> 00:56:10,860
Scott: And just keep that correct mindset when you're out in the current game world these days.

612
00:56:11,040 --> 00:56:14,460
Rym: Recognize stores, recognize grinds, recognize traps.

613
00:56:16,041 --> 00:56:29,520
Scott: And don't go to a store unless you have independently determined you need a thing, you need to shop for that thing, and you go to a store that is the best store for that thing, and avoid stores otherwise.

614
00:56:29,800 --> 00:56:40,260
Rym: And be extra fucking suspicious of stores that appear to be trying to trick you into coming, or are leveraging some sort of exclusivity or power over you.

615
00:56:46,011 --> 00:56:48,400
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

